# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — COLUMNS ARE NOT PRESERVED. A NEGATIVE, and a boundary.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_column_padding_is_not_preserved`, exact text). A sweep would
# rewrite it into its own expected output and the cell would go green-vacuous.
#
# The ruling preserves element-to-LINE ASSIGNMENT and nothing else. The author
# hand-aligned three columns below; the rows survive, the padding collapses to a
# single space after each comma.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT BOUNDARY, not a missing feature. gofmt aligns columns
# — and gofmt never rewrites the elements themselves. This formatter does:
# `!`→`^`, `( 2 )`→`(2)`, quote normalisation, radix-preserving literal
# rewrites, all riding the same sweep. Any column the author measured is
# invalidated by the canon changes travelling with it, so preserving one would
# hand the formatter a second CONTINUOUS layout dimension it cannot keep
# idempotent. Element-to-line is DISCRETE and survives element rewriting.
#
# NO PRE-FIX RED IS POSSIBLE for the padding half — the pre-change binary also
# collapses padding (it collapses the rows too). RED-verified by BREAKING the
# mechanism: teaching the emitter to reproduce the author's intra-row spacing
# makes the exact-text assertion red. The ROW half of the expected output IS
# red at HEAD — nine elements come back on nine lines.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    int y = 6
    Vector[int] xs = [
        1,   200, 30000,
        4,   5,   6,
        70,  8,   y,
    ]
    print(xs.len())
